{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "ולא תבא", :translit "v'lo tavo", :gloss "and it/they shall not come"} {:he "ולא תבא דמעתך", :translit "v'lo tavo dim'atecha", :gloss "and your tear shall not come"} {:he "ולא תבא עלינו רעה", :translit "v'lo tavo aleinu ra'ah", :gloss "and evil shall not come upon us"} {:he "ולא תבא עליו השמש", :translit "v'lo tavo alav hashemesh", :gloss "and the sun shall not come upon him"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 3, :occurrences ["ולא תבא דמעתך" "ולא תבא עלינו רעה" "ולא תבא עליו השמש"]}], :frame nil} {:quotes [{:he "והיה בך חטא", :translit "v'hayah becha chet", :gloss "and it shall be in you a sin"} {:he "לא יומתו אבות על בנים ובנים לא יומתו על אבות", :translit "lo yumu avot al banim uvanim lo yumu al avot", :gloss "fathers shall not die for children and children shall not die for fathers"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Semikhut parshiyot: the phrase והיה בך חטא is adjacent to לא יומתו אבות על בנים, yielding the inference that one's sin can cause children to die."}], :frame {:text "The principle that children may die on account of a parent's sin appears in the Talmud's discussion of the verse לא יומתו אבות על בנים: this applies only when the children do not themselves continue the sin, but when they hold the deeds of their fathers in their hands — i.e., when they persist in the same wrongdoing — they can be punished for it (Shabbat 32b). Rashi on Deuteronomy 24:16 cites this explanation from the Sifrei.", :sources ["Shabbat 32b" "Rashi on Deuteronomy 24:16" "Sifrei Deuteronomy 280"]}} {:quotes [{:he "בחטאו יומתו לא תטה", :translit "b'cheto yumu lo titteh", :gloss "in his sin he shall die — you shall not pervert"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Adjacent words בחטאו יומתו and לא תטה are read together so that the phrase 'he shall die in his sin' attaches to the prohibition 'you shall not pervert [judgment],' implying that one who perverts judgment is liable to the death penalty."}], :frame {:text "The Talmud derives that a judge who perverts judgment is subject to divine punishment of death, as stated in Shabbat 10a: 'Any judge who judges a true judgment in absolute truth becomes a partner with the Holy One; and any judge who perverts judgment is called a destroyer of God's world.' The Sifrei on Deuteronomy 16:19 likewise warns of severe consequences for judicial corruption.", :sources ["Shabbat 10a" "Sifrei Deuteronomy 144"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :masorah, :count 3, :occurrences ["ולא תבא דמעתך" "ולא תבא עלינו רעה" "ולא תבא עליו השמש"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "ולא תבא דמעתך", :refs ["Ezekiel 24:16"], :n 1, :lens :plene-insensitive} {:phrase "ולא תבא עלינו רעה", :refs ["Jeremiah 5:12" "Micah 3:11"], :n 2, :lens :plene-insensitive} {:phrase "ולא תבא עליו השמש", :refs ["Deuteronomy 24:15"], :n 1, :lens :plene-insensitive}]} {:kind :other, :note "Semikhut parshiyot: the phrase והיה בך חטא is adjacent to לא יומתו אבות על בנים, yielding the inference that one's sin can cause children to die.", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "Adjacent words בחטאו יומתו and לא תטה are read together so that the phrase 'he shall die in his sin' attaches to the prohibition 'you shall not pervert [judgment],' implying that one who perverts judgment is liable to the death penalty.", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:39:01.285272386Z", :model "glm-5.2"}